ion.\n   - If the cow is blue then the cow likes the squirrel.\n   - If the bald eagle sees the lion then the lion sees the squirrel.\n   - If someone sees the squirrel then they are green.\n   - If someone likes the bald eagle then the bald eagle is blue.\n\n2.**Identifying Relevant Information:**\n   - We need to find any direct or indirect evidence linking the bald eagle to chasing the lion.\n\n3.**Evaluating Statements:**\n   - The bald eagle chases the squirrel (not the lion).\n   - The cow chases the lion.\n   - The cow likes the bald eagle.\n   - The cow likes the lion.\n   - The lion likes the cow.\n   - The squirrel chases the bald eagle.The squirrel doesn\'t see the bald eagle.\nIf someone is not red and they don\'t chase the bald eagle then the bald eagle isn\'t green.\nIf someone dislikes the bald eagle then they dislike the lion.\nIf someone isn\'t green then they don\'t see the lion.\nIf the cow isn\'t blue then the cow dislikes the squirrel.\nIf the bald eagle doesn\'t see the lion then the lion doesn\'t see the squirrel.\nIf someone doesn\'t see the squirrel then they are not green.\nIf someone dislikes the bald eagle then the bald eagle is not blue.\n\nConclusion:\n- There is a direct statement that the bald eagle chases the lion.\n- The only relevant information about the bald eagle not chasing something is that it doesn\'t chase the squirrel.\n- The only relevant information about the lion not chasing something is that the cow doesn\'t chase the lion.\n- There is a direct link between the bald eagle and the lion in terms of chasing.\n\nTherefore, based on the given context, the statement "The bald eagle chases the lion" is **known**. There is sufficient information to determine that this statement is true.. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
